      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "The powers of p² generate the principal units modulo every 2^t: successive squaring raises the depth by one. Hence by closedness P_a⊆H. The additional coset pP_a gives 2[ℤ₂^×:H]≤φ(2^a). Since KD_A=χ₂⁻¹(H), its index in G is at most this index.",
      "review": "Independently enumerated the finite cyclotomic examples p=3,7 at depths 2 to 6. The index sequences are (1,2,2,2,2) and (1,2,4,4,4), respectively. The bounds are upper bounds for a general closed image."
    },
    {
      "id": "IOR-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Passing to the fixed abelian datum costs at most its degree in the orbit count",
      "anchor": "inputs-inertia-places",
      "equations": [
        "inputs-inertia-orbit-count"
      ],
      "statement": "For every abelian datum M with degree b and every algebraic closure Ω of ℚ, the Galois group V fixing the dyadic cyclotomic tower of M(i) acts on the prolongations of p with a cover by at most 2b or 4b orbit representatives in the two congruence classes of IOR-01. This cover projects to all finite cyclotomic layers.",
      "hypotheses": "Same two prime congruences as IOR-01. No coprimality between p and the modulus of M is needed for the orbit count itself.",
      "definitions": [
        "W=abU(M) is a normal subgroup with [G:W]=b. The group fixing all dyadic roots already fixes i, so V=W∩K.",
        "OrbLe means that there is a finite set T of places above p with bounded cardinality and that every place is vB with v∈V and B∈T.",
        "If D fixes A, K D and V D are group products since K and V are normal."
      ],
