    {
      "id": "IOR-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "One tame evaluation per orbit bounds the cost of allowing a new prime",
      "anchor": "inputs-inertia-characters",
      "equations": [
        "inputs-inertia-evaluation-equation"
      ],
      "statement": "For a prime ℓ and a subgroup V of the absolute Galois group of a number field, let C(N) be its locally constant additive 𝔽_ℓ-characters that vanish on inertia away from N. If gcd(p,ℓ)=1, the inertia groups above p lie in V, and k places cover the V-orbits above p, then |C(Np)|≤|C(N)|ℓ^k whenever C(N) is finite. For the two admissible dyadic primes this gives the exponent 4(g+6b) after both quadratic extensions.",
      "hypotheses": "The general character step uses natural N,p and an orbit cover for valuation rings A with |p|_A<1. In the application N=1 then q, and q,d are primes prime to 2m in the two required congruence classes, m the modulus of M. The initial g is fixed for M(i) before choosing q,d.",
      "definitions": [
        "A locally constant additive character of tame inertia at residue characteristic different from ℓ is detected by its value on one chosen lift γ_B inside the absolute inertia group.",
        "For two characters with equal evaluations, their difference vanishes on I_B; when A=vB, v⁻¹I_Av=I_B and v∈V.",
        "One representative is chosen per attained evaluation fibre. The injection is from the enlarged group of characters into evaluation tuples times the old group."
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "IOR-02",
        "QCS-02",
        "TIC-02"
      ],
